each other's respective rights and privileges are an essential ingredient in the relationship between Parliament and 
the courts. In the circumstances, Mr. Goonesekere submitted, the Court ought to have sought the assistance of the 
Attorney-General in deciding a matter that impinged on parliamentary privilege. That was, he said, the 122 invariable 
practice in England, as numerous decisions of the courts of that country showed. Mr. Goonesekere quoted the following 
words from the judgment of Popplewell, J. in Rost (supra) at p 644 to illustrate his submission: It became clear after 
the initial submissions of counsel that the question of parliamentary privilege might be involved and counsel agreed 
that the only course open to the court was to adjourn further argument and to set out the matters which might give 
rise to parliamentary privilege in writing; then to submit those questions to the Attorney-General and the court to 
